1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,029
Which route would you choose for this packet?
The one with

2
00:00:05,029 --> 00:00:10,057
three connections looks shorter than the
winding one with five. But

3
00:00:10,057 --> 00:00:15,086
a network does not pay for how tidy a route
looks

4
00:00:15,086 --> 00:00:20,114
on a drawing. It pays the cost of every
connection it

5
00:00:20,114 --> 00:00:25,143
uses. In a moment, that longer-looking path
will beat the shortcut.

6
00:00:25,143 --> 00:00:30,171
The interesting question is how to discover it
without trying every

7
00:00:30,171 --> 00:00:35,200
possible journey. That is the puzzle
Dijkstra's algorithm solves.

8
00:00:35,200 --> 00:00:40,038
Here is our small world: six points, nine
connections, and a

9
00:00:40,038 --> 00:00:44,876
positive number on each connection. A point
could represent a router,

10
00:00:44,876 --> 00:00:49,714
but the algorithm only needs the graph. The
numbers are illustrative

11
00:00:49,714 --> 00:00:54,552
costs, not automatically seconds or
kilometers. We choose what they mean

12
00:00:54,552 --> 00:00:59,390
before asking for the cheapest route. Keep
your eye on A,

13
00:00:59,390 --> 00:01:04,229
our starting point, and F, our destination.
Everything else is a

14
00:01:04,229 --> 00:01:09,067
possible intermediate step in their journey.

15
00:01:09,067 --> 00:01:13,971
Follow A to B, B to D, then D to F.

16
00:01:13,971 --> 00:01:18,876
The costs are four, five, and six, totaling
fifteen. Now take

17
00:01:18,876 --> 00:01:23,781
A to C to B to D to E to F.

18
00:01:23,781 --> 00:01:28,686
Two, one, five, two, three: thirteen. More
connections, smaller bill. Try

19
00:01:28,686 --> 00:01:33,590
predicting the winner from the picture alone
and it is easy

20
00:01:33,590 --> 00:01:38,495
to get fooled. The numbers describe our
objective, so the complete

21
00:01:38,495 --> 00:01:43,400
sum must decide which route is better.

22
00:01:43,400 --> 00:01:48,305
Instead of guessing a complete route, we will
grow a region

23
00:01:48,305 --> 00:01:53,210
of known shortest distances. A starts at zero
because reaching the

24
00:01:53,210 --> 00:01:58,114
place where we already are costs nothing.
Every other point begins

25
00:01:58,114 --> 00:02:03,019
at infinity, our shorthand for no route
discovered yet. These are

26
00:02:03,019 --> 00:02:07,924
distance estimates, not numbers written on the
links. The central rule

27
00:02:07,924 --> 00:02:12,829
is simple: choose the smallest unfinished
estimate, settle it, and check

28
00:02:12,829 --> 00:02:17,733
whether going through it improves any
neighbor.

29
00:02:17,733 --> 00:02:22,829
A is the only point with a finite estimate, so
it

30
00:02:22,829 --> 00:02:27,924
is settled first. Its connection to B offers a
distance of

31
00:02:27,924 --> 00:02:33,019
four; its connection to C offers two. Write
both as tentative

32
00:02:33,019 --> 00:02:38,114
results. Tentative matters: discovering a
route to B does not mean

33
00:02:38,114 --> 00:02:43,210
we have discovered the cheapest route to B. We
have only

34
00:02:43,210 --> 00:02:48,305
found the best one so far. Which unfinished
point should we

35
00:02:48,305 --> 00:02:53,400
explore next? Choose the smallest estimate,
not the closest-looking dot.

36
00:02:53,400 --> 00:02:58,238
C wins with distance two. Explore its
connections and something useful

37
00:02:58,238 --> 00:03:03,076
happens. Through C, reaching B costs two plus
one: three. That

38
00:03:03,076 --> 00:03:07,914
improves B's earlier four, so replace the
estimate and remember C

39
00:03:07,914 --> 00:03:12,752
as B's predecessor. Through C, we also
discover D at ten

40
00:03:12,752 --> 00:03:17,590
and E at twelve. This repeated operation is
called relaxation. The

41
00:03:17,590 --> 00:03:22,429
word sounds restful, but the job is simply
comparing an old

42
00:03:22,429 --> 00:03:27,267
estimate with a newly discovered candidate.

43
00:03:27,267 --> 00:03:32,295
Now B has the smallest unfinished distance:
three. Its connection to

44
00:03:32,295 --> 00:03:37,324
D costs five. Add that connection to the known
cost of

45
00:03:37,324 --> 00:03:42,352
reaching B, and the candidate for D becomes
eight. Eight beats

46
00:03:42,352 --> 00:03:47,381
ten, so update D and save B as its
predecessor. Notice

47
00:03:47,381 --> 00:03:52,410
what the calculation includes. We are not
choosing the cheapest outgoing

48
00:03:52,410 --> 00:03:57,438
connection in isolation. Every candidate
includes the entire cost already spent

49
00:03:57,438 --> 00:04:02,467
getting to the point where this new step
begins.

50
00:04:02,467 --> 00:04:07,495
D is next at eight. Through D, E improves from
twelve

51
00:04:07,495 --> 00:04:12,524
to ten, and F appears with distance fourteen.
We have reached

52
00:04:12,524 --> 00:04:17,552
the destination in our search. Should we stop?
Hold that thought.

53
00:04:17,552 --> 00:04:22,581
Fourteen is still an unfinished estimate, and
E is waiting with

54
00:04:22,581 --> 00:04:27,610
a smaller distance of ten. If the remaining
link from E

55
00:04:27,610 --> 00:04:32,638
offers a better total, stopping now would miss
it. Discovering the

56
00:04:32,638 --> 00:04:37,667
destination is different from proving its
distance is final.

57
00:04:37,667 --> 00:04:42,633
Settle E at ten. Its connection to F costs
three, making

58
00:04:42,633 --> 00:04:47,600
thirteen. That beats F's fourteen, so the
estimate changes and its

59
00:04:47,600 --> 00:04:52,567
predecessor becomes E. This is the same small
operation we have

60
00:04:52,567 --> 00:04:57,533
used all along: known distance plus link cost,
compared with the

61
00:04:57,533 --> 00:05:02,500
current estimate. There is no special clever
trick for the destination.

62
00:05:02,500 --> 00:05:07,467
The repeated local calculation is enough to
uncover a complete route

63
00:05:07,467 --> 00:05:12,433
that our first visual guess failed to
recognize.

64
00:05:12,433 --> 00:05:17,462
F now has the smallest unfinished estimate,
thirteen, so settle it.

65
00:05:17,462 --> 00:05:22,490
Our final distances from A are zero for A,
three for

66
00:05:22,490 --> 00:05:27,519
B, two for C, eight for D, ten for E, and

67
00:05:27,519 --> 00:05:32,548
thirteen for F. Watch the green settled region
finish growing. Each

68
00:05:32,548 --> 00:05:37,576
number was earned by a comparison, rather than
copied from a

69
00:05:37,576 --> 00:05:42,605
completed answer. The destination's cost is
now final because it has

70
00:05:42,605 --> 00:05:47,633
crossed the same settled boundary as the
earlier points.

71
00:05:47,633 --> 00:05:52,200
Why can that boundary be trusted? Imagine a
cheaper undiscovered route

72
00:05:52,200 --> 00:05:56,767
to the point we are about to settle. It must
leave

73
00:05:56,767 --> 00:06:01,333
our settled region somewhere. But our frontier
already contains the cheapest

74
00:06:01,333 --> 00:06:05,900
available unfinished estimate, and every
remaining connection has a nonnegative cost.

75
00:06:05,900 --> 00:06:10,467
Continuing farther cannot secretly subtract
distance. That is the intuition behind

76
00:06:10,467 --> 00:06:15,033
the rule. Negative links would break this
argument, which is why

77
00:06:15,033 --> 00:06:19,600
the graph's cost assumptions are part of the
algorithm, not fine

78
00:06:19,600 --> 00:06:24,167
print.

79
00:06:24,167 --> 00:06:28,792
We have a cost, but a packet needs a route.
Each

80
00:06:28,792 --> 00:06:33,417
improvement saved a predecessor: the point
that gave us the better

81
00:06:33,417 --> 00:06:38,042
estimate. Begin at F and follow those clues
backward: E, D,

82
00:06:38,042 --> 00:06:42,667
B, C, A. Reverse that chain and the forward
route appears.

83
00:06:42,667 --> 00:06:47,292
The distance number tells us how much the best
journey costs;

84
00:06:47,292 --> 00:06:51,917
the predecessor chain tells us which journey
achieved it. Without those

85
00:06:51,917 --> 00:06:56,542
saved clues, the cheapest total alone would
not draw the path

86
00:06:56,542 --> 00:07:01,167
for us.

87
00:07:01,167 --> 00:07:06,195
Now let the packet travel from A through C, B,
D,

88
00:07:06,195 --> 00:07:11,224
E, and finally F. Add each link as it moves:
two,

89
00:07:11,224 --> 00:07:16,252
three, eight, ten, thirteen. The arithmetic
and the picture describe the

90
00:07:16,252 --> 00:07:21,281
same thing at different scales. A only needs C
as its

91
00:07:21,281 --> 00:07:26,310
immediate next step toward this destination.
We are showing a route

92
00:07:26,310 --> 00:07:31,338
on a small teaching graph; actual routing
systems apply their own

93
00:07:31,338 --> 00:07:36,367
rules when they turn such calculations into
forwarding decisions.

94
00:07:36,367 --> 00:07:41,524
Suppose the connection between D and E fails.
Our thirteen-cost route

95
00:07:41,524 --> 00:07:46,681
no longer exists. Keeping the old answer would
be wonderfully efficient

96
00:07:46,681 --> 00:07:51,838
right up until the packet reaches the missing
connection. So change

97
00:07:51,838 --> 00:07:56,995
the graph and ask the same question again. We
are not

98
00:07:56,995 --> 00:08:02,152
changing the meaning of cheapest, and we do
not need a

99
00:08:02,152 --> 00:08:07,310
new algorithm. We need to run the calculation
against the connections

100
00:08:07,310 --> 00:08:12,467
that are actually available now, rather than
the ones we remember.

101
00:08:12,467 --> 00:08:17,092
Without that connection, A to C to B to D to

102
00:08:17,092 --> 00:08:21,717
F costs two plus one plus five plus six:
fourteen. The

103
00:08:21,717 --> 00:08:26,342
route through C and E costs two plus ten plus
three:

104
00:08:26,342 --> 00:08:30,967
fifteen. Fourteen wins. The updated distances
keep E at twelve and

105
00:08:30,967 --> 00:08:35,592
reach F at fourteen. A failure does not mean
every earlier

106
00:08:35,592 --> 00:08:40,217
number must change, but none should be trusted
merely because it

107
00:08:40,217 --> 00:08:44,842
appeared in yesterday's answer. The graph
determines what the calculation can

108
00:08:44,842 --> 00:08:49,467
justify today.

109
00:08:49,467 --> 00:08:54,033
Return to the two routes we started with. The
shortest-looking line

110
00:08:54,033 --> 00:08:58,600
was never the right test. The useful question
was which complete

111
00:08:58,600 --> 00:09:03,167
journey had the lowest sum, and Dijkstra
answered by growing a

112
00:09:03,167 --> 00:09:07,733
trustworthy boundary of distances. Small
comparisons produced a global result, then

113
00:09:07,733 --> 00:09:12,300
the same method adapted to a changed
connection. That is the

114
00:09:12,300 --> 00:09:16,867
satisfying idea to keep: we did not need to
guess the

115
00:09:16,867 --> 00:09:21,433
whole winning route. We built enough correct
local knowledge to reveal

116
00:09:21,433 --> 00:09:26,000
it.
